From 447305afddfa09ae614957cd06658a2f7bfdf215 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:29:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the Dolby Vision signal goes out with the
 change of mode, before the first picture

The change of mode that VideoPlayer makes for a video that goes out natively was held for the
first frame of native output, which carried it with the transport in one commit (CModeChangeHold,
START), and VideoPlayer and the audio engine were told of the change with that commit. By then
VideoPlayer had decoded and queued the first pictures and the audio engine had opened its HDMI
sink for the stream. Told then, VideoPlayer pauses its clock and flushes its renderer, which drops
the pictures queued after the first one, and the audio engine closes the sink it had just opened
and opens it again once the display is back. In the test of 2026-10-28 (RTX 4080 SUPER, LG C5, a
profile 7 stream with TrueHD from a server on the Internet): the change of mode chosen at 0 s, the
passthrough sink open at 0.37 s, the first picture at 0.59 s, the commit of the mode with the
transport at 0.92 s, the sink open again at 1.53 s.

Now that commit is made when the change of mode is (CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::DVStartSignal()):
native output begins, a transport frame of black with valid metadata is drawn
(CDVBridgeGLES::DrawStartSignal()) and committed with the mode of the video, the commit that the
first frame would have made, told as the change of mode that it is: the display lost before it,
back once the delay that the user set has passed. VideoPlayer and the audio engine are told before
the decoder and the audio stream open, as they are of every other change of the refresh rate in
Kodi, and the display sets itself up while the video opens. The renderer of the video takes the
output as it takes it from a video before (CDVModeMove::HandsOver()): its first frame is one more
frame of the transport, in a commit with no modeset. The mode is the one Kodi chose: the cadence
does not change.

Only where nothing is in doubt (CDVStartSignal::Refuses()): VideoPlayer told of the change for a
video that goes out natively, of profile 5, 7, 8.1 or 8.6 (not 8.2 or 8.4, whose composition each
picture's RPU decides, nor AV1); the change is one that could be held (atomic modesetting, the
output on, the same window, no 3D, another mode, not a link brought up from scratch); the mode
carries the transport at a rate that the display takes for native output; native output is Dolby
Vision Standard (not Low-Latency, not the HDMI 1.4b signalling) and is not disabled after a
failure; no video has its output set up; nothing else holds or keeps the output, and the GUI is
not composed over HDR; the window is that of the transport (3840x2160, 8 bits, OpenGL ES 3).
Anything else: the hold, as before.

Fail closed. Where native output cannot begin, the frame cannot be drawn or the mode cannot be
selected, the capture is let go without a commit and the change is held as before. Where the
commit is refused, the ordinary output is restored with the mode of the video in its commit, and
the video begins native output with its first frame, as after a first frame that the driver
refused. Either way the signal is not set up so again until Kodi is started again. The output
waits for the video while the player plays, 10 s at most; where the player stops first, the time
passes, or the renderer of the video does not take it (it does not draw natively, or makes another
surface), the ordinary output is restored, in one commit with the mode of the GUI where Kodi sets
one; two videos in a row that do not take it, and it is not set up again until Kodi is started
again. With NVIDIA the commit is the one that the first frame made (the mode and the transport,
the head not turned off), and the end of the video is restored as before, in one commit with the
mode that follows; with amdgpu and i915 the same commits as before, earlier.

kodi.log says when it was used and the times: the change of mode with the signal (packets, and
how long after the change was asked for), the display back for VideoPlayer and the audio engine,
the renderer of the video that took it, and its first frame (how long after the signal, and whether
its commit changed nothing but the picture); and why where it was not used or did not work.
<video><dvstartsignal>false</dvstartsignal></video> in advancedsettings.xml turns it off, to
compare (the change waits for the first frame, as before).

Idea from YBLOD 0.3.0 (its native startup handoff, which commits a black transport frame during
the refresh change; measured by them on an N150 with an LG B4: no pictures left out at the start
instead of one or two, and HDMI audio back 0.6 s after opening instead of 2.1 s), read as data and
written again for Hades: through the transport, the commit and the hand-over of native output as
they are, the metadata through the serializer of the renderer, and only where everything is
eligible.

The decisions have a host test (65 checks, 23 mutations caught). The rest runs only with a GPU and
a display: not run on hardware.
